@anon.3c5d4856225436a016f648d8ca8332b3.3240 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [4299 x i8] }> <{ [4299 x i8] c"# `unsized_locals`\0A\0AThe tracking issue for this feature is: [#48055]\0A\0A[#48055]: https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/48055\0A\0A------------------------\0A\0AThis implements [RFC1909]. When turned on, you can have unsized arguments and locals:\0A\0A[RFC1909]: https://github.com/rust-lang/rfcs/blob/master/text/1909-unsized-rvalues.md\0A\0A```rust\0A#![allow(incomplete_features)]\0A#![feature(unsized_locals, unsized_fn_params)]\0A\0Ause std::any::Any;\0A\0Afn main() {\0A    let x: Box<dyn Any> = Box::new(42);\0A    let x: dyn Any = *x;\0A    //  ^ unsized local variable\0A    //               ^^ unsized temporary\0A    foo(x);\0A}\0A\0Afn foo(_: dyn Any) {}\0A//     ^^^^^^ unsized argument\0A```\0A\0AThe RFC still forbids the following unsized expressions:\0A\0A```rust,compile_fail\0A#![feature(unsized_locals)]\0A\0Ause std::any::Any;\0A\0Astruct MyStruct<T: ?Sized> {\0A    content: T,\0A}\0A\0Astruct MyTupleStruct<T: ?Sized>(T);\0A\0Afn answer() -> Box<dyn Any> {\0A    Box::new(42)\0A}\0A\0Afn main() {\0A    // You CANNOT have unsized statics.\0A    static X: dyn Any = *answer();  // ERROR\0A    const Y: dyn Any = *answer();  // ERROR\0A\0A    // You CANNOT have struct initialized unsized.\0A    MyStruct { content: *answer() };  // ERROR\0A    MyTupleStruct(*answer());  // ERROR\0A    (42, *answer());  // ERROR\0A\0A    // You CANNOT have unsized return types.\0A    fn my_function() -> dyn Any { *answer() }  // ERROR\0A\0A    // You CAN have unsized local variables...\0A    let mut x: dyn Any = *answer();  // OK\0A    // ...but you CANNOT reassign to them.\0A    x = *answer();  // ERROR\0A\0A    // You CANNOT even initialize them separately.\0A    let y: dyn Any;  // OK\0A    y = *answer();  // ERROR\0A\0A    // Not mentioned in the RFC, but by-move captured variables are also Sized.\0A    let x: dyn Any = *answer();\0A    (move || {  // ERROR\0A        let y = x;\0A    })();\0A\0A    // You CAN create a closure with unsized arguments,\0A    // but you CANNOT call it.\0A    // This is an implementation detail and may be changed in the future.\0A    let f = |x: dyn Any| {};\0A    f(*answer());  // ERROR\0A}\0A```\0A\0A## By-value trait objects\0A\0AWith this feature, you can have by-value `self` arguments without `Self: Sized` bounds.\0A\0A```rust\0A#![feature(unsized_fn_params)]\0A\0Atrait Foo {\0A    fn foo(self) {}\0A}\0A\0Aimpl<T: ?Sized> Foo for T {}\0A\0Afn main() {\0A    let slice: Box<[i32]> = Box::new([1, 2, 3]);\0A    <[i32] as Foo>::foo(*slice);\0A}\0A```\0A\0AAnd `Foo` will also be object-safe.\0A\0A```rust\0A#![feature(unsized_fn_params)]\0A\0Atrait Foo {\0A    fn foo(self) {}\0A}\0A\0Aimpl<T: ?Sized> Foo for T {}\0A\0Afn main () {\0A    let slice: Box<dyn Foo> = Box::new([1, 2, 3]);\0A    // doesn't compile yet\0A    <dyn Foo as Foo>::foo(*slice);\0A}\0A```\0A\0AOne of the objectives of this feature is to allow `Box<dyn FnOnce>`.\0A\0A## Variable length arrays\0A\0AThe RFC also describes an extension to the array literal syntax: `[e; dyn n]`. In the syntax, `n` isn't necessarily a constant expression. The array is dynamically allocated on the stack and has the type of `[T]`, instead of `[T; n]`.\0A\0A```rust,ignore (not-yet-implemented)\0A#![feature(unsized_locals)]\0A\0Afn mergesort<T: Ord>(a: &mut [T]) {\0A    let mut tmp = [T; dyn a.len()];\0A    // ...\0A}\0A\0Afn main() {\0A    let mut a = [3, 1, 5, 6];\0A    mergesort(&mut a);\0A    assert_eq!(a, [1, 3, 5, 6]);\0A}\0A```\0A\0AVLAs are not implemented yet. The syntax isn't final, either. We may need an alternative syntax for Rust 2015 because, in Rust 2015, expressions like `[e; dyn(1)]` would be ambiguous. One possible alternative proposed in the RFC is `[e; n]`: if `n` captures one or more local variables, then it is considered as `[e; dyn n]`.\0A\0A## Advisory on stack usage\0A\0AIt's advised not to casually use the `#![feature(unsized_locals)]` feature. Typical use-cases are:\0A\0A- When you need a by-value trait objects.\0A- When you really need a fast allocation of small temporary arrays.\0A\0AAnother pitfall is repetitive allocation and temporaries. Currently the compiler simply extends the stack frame every time it encounters an unsized assignment. So for example, the code\0A\0A```rust\0A#![feature(unsized_locals)]\0A\0Afn main() {\0A    let x: Box<[i32]> = Box::new([1, 2, 3, 4, 5]);\0A    let _x = {{{{{{{{{{*x}}}}}}}}}};\0A}\0A```\0A\0Aand the code\0A\0A```rust\0A#![feature(unsized_locals)]\0A\0Afn main() {\0A    for _ in 0..10 {\0A        let x: Box<[i32]> = Box::new([1, 2, 3, 4, 5]);\0A        let _x = *x;\0A    }\0A}\0A```\0A\0Awill unnecessarily extend the stack frame.\0A" }>, align 1
